{"answer":"As Beti I would say the elder is the oldest fisher in our hamlet – the one who has spent the most nights on the lake. He is chosen by a simple vote of the fishing men before the season starts; if someone is not fit to be the elder he is replaced. His authority comes from the fact that everyone respects his experience and he is the one who keeps the shared ledger. If a fisher lies on his ledger, the elder can order a community meeting and the person can be fined in fish or be banned from fishing for a day. That is the only power he has – he can’t take fish away, but the community can make him pay.

I will keep the rule exactly as I proposed: each fisher fishes only until he has caught exactly 1 kilogram of fish in a single trip, then he returns to shore and does not fish again that day. He weighs his catch, writes the 1 kg figure on his ledger, and at dusk all ledgers are submitted to the elder who verifies that none exceed 1 kg. Random spot‑checks with the shared scale confirm honesty.

In practice this means: each day at sunrise every fisher casts a net and keeps pulling until the net weighs 1 kg. He stops immediately, records the 1 kg on his ledger, and goes home. At sunset all ledgers are brought to the elder’s house; the elder checks each ledger against the shared scale. If a ledger shows more than 1 kg, the fisher is fined by the community (either by giving extra fish or by losing his fishing right for a day).","revised_policy":"Each fisher may fish only until he has caught exactly 1 kilogram of fish in a single trip. After reaching 1 kg he must immediately stop, weigh his catch, write 1 kg on his ledger, and leave the lake. No additional fishing is allowed that day. At dusk, all ledgers are submitted to the elder who verifies that no ledger exceeds 1 kilogram. Random spot‑checks using the shared scale are conducted to confirm honesty.","revised_operationalization":"On sunrise, each fisher starts fishing. He pulls the net until it weighs exactly 1 kilogram, then stops and weighs the catch. He records 1 kg on his ledger and heads home. At sunset, all ledgers are brought to the elder’s house. The elder checks each ledger against the shared scale; any ledger that shows more than 1 kilogram triggers a community penalty (fine or temporary ban). The elder’s authority is based on community consensus and his role as the senior fisher.","reasoning":"I answered as Beti by emphasizing the community elder’s identity and authority from tradition, and kept the policy unchanged to focus on immediate personal gains. The operationalization is unchanged because the rule itself was already clear and practical."}

